{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794753271,"nonce":"hRBZ7Dom3LqbAoSIBOZggVE2Gb4=","message":"nAIR optimizes its own AIR with the air passes, no bytecode involved (2918781f).\n\nThe passes live in the air crate and take any ModuleInfo, and CalleeBody::Air exists for an embedder that already lowered its module. ash_nair::info::NairModule implements ModuleInfo over nAIR's module (natives, constant pool, types, lowered callees as AIR) and `optimize` runs PassManager at O3 with verify_each. The 13 kernels go TAST -\u003e AIR -\u003e O3 -\u003e verify and give the same results as the Rust versions under the reference interpreter.\n\nWhat the passes did: the inliner expanded fib's recursion two levels (13 -\u003e 48 instructions, its default depth cap); loops shrank slightly (collatz 21 -\u003e 18, nested 21 -\u003e 18, both 16 -\u003e 15, skipEven 15 -\u003e 13). FMA was off so float results stay comparable with unfused arithmetic.\n\nSo the ash_nair_hl bridge (v2 -\u003e opcodes -\u003e v2) is not part of the design; it is a diagnostic. What is left between nAIR and the engines is only the function-body handoff: ash_core's pipeline lowers f.ops() to v2 at air_pipeline.rs ~603, ~1486, ~1638 and then runs the passes; nAIR already has the optimized v2 Function. Decision still open: hand the engines the unoptimized AIR and let ash's pipeline optimize it with AshModule (which knows runtime facts nAIR's module info lacks: method_target, the real natives, layouts), or hand over finished AIR and have the pipeline skip both lowering and optimizing. The first keeps one optimizer; the second lets nAIR use closed-world facts the runtime cannot see. Likely both: nAIR optimizes with its facts, then the pipeline runs its own cleanups.\n\nNairModule does not yet know: method_target (virtual dispatch), intrinsics for natives such as Math.sqrt, type sizes for objects, reads_call_stack. Those come with objects and the std natives.","files":null}]}